WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.233
Biomedical ceramic design starts with a difficult demand: a

00:00:05.233 --> 00:00:09.915
manufactured structure must remain useful in a biological environment.

00:00:13.842 --> 00:00:18.905
Additive manufacturing can produce complex ceramic forms. Processing, internal

00:00:18.905 --> 00:00:23.967
architecture and material properties shape what those forms can do.

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:32.489
Ceramic processing and densification impose constraints beyond the

00:00:32.489 --> 00:00:36.845
original digital model. The final properties must be measured.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:45.666
Mechanical performance and biological response can pull design in different

00:00:45.666 --> 00:00:50.781
directions. An elegant lattice is not automatically a medical device.

00:00:54.925 --> 00:01:00.083
Clinical application also depends on repeatability, sterilisation and

00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:05.241
regulatory pathways; each requires evidence distinct from printability.

00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:14.805
The accepted chapter reviews biomedical ceramic applications and translational constraints.

00:01:14.805 --> 00:01:20.226
It is an editorial explanation, not a new implantation trial.
